# Line endings are pinned here rather than left to each machine core.autocrlf.
#
# The default puts every text file in LF, in the blob and in the working tree, on
# every platform. The blobs were already LF throughout - every text file in the
# index, mvnw.cmd included - so this keeps them that way rather than changing them.
# What it changes is the checkout: without it a Windows clone writes CRLF into the
# working tree, and a byte-exact comparison against content written by a tool that
# emits LF then fails on the line endings alone. A proof anchor bent exactly that
# way while a test was being verified.
#
# *.cmd stays CRLF because Windows batch files require it. Its blob is LF like every
# other text file here; only its checkout is CRLF, and that behaviour is unchanged.
#
# The two narrower LF rules are redundant under the default. They stay as written
# intent, naming the files where the ending is load-bearing rather than incidental.
#
# The binary markers declare what git content detection would conclude anyway, so no
# heuristic is load-bearing for the images.

* text=auto eol=lf
/mvnw text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
.github/scripts/**/*.sh text eol=lf
*.png binary
*.ico binary
